## interface extends interface

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}

interface Admin extends User {
  role: 'admin' | 'superadmin';
}
// Admin has id, name, and role
```

`extends` copies every member of the base and adds the new ones.

## Extending multiple interfaces

One interface can extend several at once, merging them all:

```ts
interface Timestamped { createdAt: Date }
interface Identifiable { id: string }

interface Entity extends Timestamped, Identifiable {
  name: string;
}
```

If two bases declare the same property with incompatible types, that is a compile error.

## Extending classes and type aliases

An interface can also extend a class (inheriting its instance shape) or a type alias, as long as the alias resolves to an object type:

```ts
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
interface Point3D extends Point { z: number }
```

## interface extends vs type intersection

The `type` equivalent is intersection with `&`:

```ts
type Admin = User & { role: string };
```

Both express "everything in `User`, plus more". The differences: `interface extends` produces clearer error messages, supports declaration merging, and caches slightly better. `&` is more flexible — it composes unions, mapped types, and primitives too. For plain object inheritance, `interface extends` is the more readable default.

