## Both narrow access from public

```ts
class Base {
  private secret = 1;
  protected shared = 2;
  public open = 3;
}
```

## private — declaring class only

A `private` member is reachable only inside the class that declares it. Not subclasses, not instances elsewhere:

```ts
class Child extends Base {
  read() {
    return this.secret; // Error — private to Base
  }
}
```

## protected — class and subclasses

A `protected` member is reachable inside the declaring class and any subclass, but still not from outside:

```ts
class Child extends Base {
  read() {
    return this.shared; // OK — protected is visible to subclasses
  }
}

new Child().shared; // Error — not accessible on an instance from outside
```

## Choosing between them

- `private` — the member is an implementation detail even subclasses should not depend on. Maximum encapsulation; you can refactor it freely.
- `protected` — the member is a deliberate extension point: a base class sharing internal state or a helper method with the classes built on it.

Default to `private` and promote to `protected` only when a subclass genuinely needs the access. A widely-used `protected` member is part of your inheritance contract and harder to change later.

## Compile-time only

Both are erased at build. For runtime-enforced privacy use a `#` field — but `#` is strictly class-only, with no `protected` equivalent.

